prompt:
System instructions:
You will receive a codebook and a set of press releases from US senators.

Following the codebook, classify each text according to whether the senator is "credit-claiming." Use only the information provided in the codebook, and no other information.

Use only one of these labels:

"1": The senator is credit-claiming.
"0": The senator is not credit-claiming.

The documents will be provided as JSON objects with an ID and text:

[
  {"document_id": "doc_001", "text": "..."},
  {"document_id": "doc_002", "text": "..."},
  ...
]

For each text, return:
* ID: ID of the text document
* Label: Your classification, 1 or 0
* Explanation: An explanation of the rationale for your classification. Explain which parts of the codebook's clarification or negative clarification you relied on the make your decision. If you faced any ambiguities or difficulties in making a classification, you can describe that here.

Return a JSON object matching the supplied output format schema.



The codebook is provided below, formatted as a JSON object.

Apply the codebook independently to every document.

{
  "definition": "'Credit-claiming' is defined as a statement in which a legislator asserts personal or collective responsibility for successfully securing a specific, tangible government action, benefit, grant, or legislative outcome for their constituents or the nation.",
  "clarification": "This includes announcing the award of federal grants or resources to local entities, securing specific earmarks or federal funding, explicitly mentioning the passage of a sponsored bill or amendment, successfully influencing an agency decision (bureaucratic casework with concrete results), or obtaining a facility/project for the district. The text must explicitly state or strongly imply that the senator's actions (e.g., authoring the provision, requesting the funds, writing a letter, brokering an agreement) were instrumental in achieving the tangible outcome. Mentions of past successful legislative achievements also qualify.",
  "positive_examples": [
    {
      "text": "U S Senator Charles E Schumer today announced that Westchester County has received two federal grants totaling 134,004... This federal money will help make sure that firefighters in Westchester County get the resources they need to keep us safe Schumer was among the first senators who recognized the need for the federal government to establish a funding program...",
      "explanation": "The senator is announcing a concrete financial benefit to their state and specifically points out their personal role in pushing for and establishing the funding. This represents a classic form of pork-barrel credit-claiming."
    },
    {
      "text": "SENATE ADOPTS NELSON AMENDMENT TO PROVIDE FAIRNESS FOR U S CATTLE PRODUCERS... Nebraska s Senator Ben Nelson today said he was gratified that his amendment to prevent the USDA from allowing some Japanese beef imports... passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26.",
      "explanation": "The senator highlights the successful passage of an amendment he personally authored, demonstrating personal efficacy in producing a desired legislative outcome."
    },
    {
      "text": "Administrator finally responds to Sen Landrieu s letter in testimony... Dr McClellan said the more than 250 000 Louisiana seniors... will now have until December 31 2006 to file without penalty... I am pleased that the agency has finally come to its senses and recognized the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention last month.",
      "explanation": "The senator takes credit for influencing a bureaucratic agency's decision, emphasizing that her prior direct pressure (a letter) yielded a tangible, desired outcome for her constituents."
    }
  ],
  "negative_clarification": "Texts should NOT be classified as credit-claiming if they merely involve 'position taking' (expressing support or opposition for a policy without claiming a concrete achievement), announcing the mere introduction or co-sponsorship of a bill (without indicating it has passed or reached a major milestone like committee passage), criticizing political opponents or agencies, giving broad policy speeches, holding hearings, or announcing routine town hall meetings ('advertising').",
  "negative_examples": [
    {
      "text": "Senate Majority Leader Sen. Harry Reid released the following statement today in response to President Bush's call for a global greenhouse-gases emissions goal: The Bush Administration's 'head in the sand' position on global warming has defied reason and common sense.",
      "explanation": "This text exemplifies position taking and blame-generating. The senator is criticizing an opponent's policy without claiming credit for any personal, tangible achievements or securing particularized benefits."
    },
    {
      "text": "Today Senator Edward M Kennedy introduced legislation to enhance stem cell research and to strengthen anti cloning provisions... The misguided fears of today can t be allowed to deny the cures of tomorrow...",
      "explanation": "This is a bill introduction. While the senator is taking an action, they are merely proposing legislation rather than announcing its successful passage or the actual securing of tangible benefits."
    },
    {
      "text": "U S Senator R Colorado will hold a town meeting in Greeley on Wednesday January 12 2005 from 10 30 a m to 11 30 a m... This is a chance for citizens to visit one on one with me about the issues that are important to them...",
      "explanation": "This is a form of advertising. The senator is announcing their availability to constituents rather than taking credit for a specific government action, grant, or legislative achievement."
    }
  ]
}
